why 2 1/2 LEADING_TONE The bass walks down B♭–A–G, a descending line, so its A continues down to G. The B♭ that A leads to is already in the soprano.
why 3 0 CROSSING The alto drops to C below the tenor's F and stays there for a beat while the tune moves A–B♭–A. The inner voices trade places so each holds a steady note.
why 3 1/4 CROSSING The alto holds C under the tenor's F, still crossed.
why 3 3/8 CROSSING Still crossed; on the next eighth the tenor falls to A and the order is back.
why 4 1/4 SPACING The alto climbs G–A–B♭–C a third under the soprano while the tenor stays low near the bass. For a beat the upper pair sings as a duet.
why 4 3/8 SPACING The same duet: alto on A, tenor low on E♭.
why 4 1/2 SPACING Still open, alto on B♭ and tenor on F.
why 4 5/8 SPACING The widest point; on the next beat alto and tenor meet again on A and F.
why 9 1/4 DOUBLING A passing chord over the held bass D; its doubled B♭ follows the alto and tenor lines.
why 9 1/4 SIX_FOUR Over the bass's held D the tune climbs to G and the inner voices move to B♭: a passing ⁶₄ on the way to IV.
why 11 1/2 SEVENTH The soprano climbs G–A–B♭ in the tune and the tenor leaps G–E♭ into a diminished seventh chord. The G's belonged to a passing chord over the bass's E, so they are not sevenths that must fall.
why 11 1/2 CROSSING The tenor leaps up to E♭ above the alto's C, and both fall by step into G minor (E♭–D, C–B♭). For a beat the tenor is on top.
why 11 1/2 CROSS_RELATION The bass's E♮, then E♭ in the tenor: the music turns toward G minor. The E♭ is the seventh of the diminished chord and falls to D, so the ear follows it in the tenor's line.
why 12 0 CROSSING Still crossed: tenor D above alto B♭ in the G minor chord. On the next beat the alto leaps up to F and the order is back.
why 13 0 DIRECT The tune falls E♭–C while the bass leaps B♭–F for the final dominant: a hidden fifth at a cadence, where the tune's line is given.
